she grew up in Minneapolis she's a  member of both the Ojibwe and Dakota  tribes and because she didn't grow up on  their reservation she has sometimes  wondered if she can be the authentic  voice for the stories that she most  wants to tell but I think that when you  get a chance to read her screenplays or  said girls you will agree with these  judges that Winona is the true voice  that we've been waiting for I do think  it's really common for writers to ask  themselves I might the one to tell the  story do I even know how it's a very  common feeling when you begin a new  screenplay but for one none of that  sense of uncertainty I think was  compounded because she had already  abandoned a dream when she was young she  dropped out of school got married had  three kids left her life left her kids  but there was a little voice in her that  said there has to be more from me and at  30 in a restless moment she picked up a  book on screenwriting and a whole world  opened for her and building on nothing  more than her love for movies for being  a watcher of movies Winona began writing  I do think that writing can be learned  but I don't really think that it can be  taught I think that we are all  essentially autodidact we write badly at  first we write clumsily it's very  humbling screenwriting seems like  something that anyone can do until you  try to do it  [Laughter]  and Wynonna wrote numerous screenplays  as she worked to develop her gifts as a  screenwriter she applied to the Academy  nickel a lot of times blind faith I  think is the first prerequisite for a  person who's teaching themselves to  write and I am really glad that Winona  kept the faith in herself through 22  screenplays that she wrote before she  rose to this  wenona I just want to say that your  apprenticeship to all of the movies that  you watched and studied has really paid  off and I can safely attest that you are  really a real screenwriter and I hope  there is a producer out here who's a  student enough to option your screenplay  that it's the right producer and that  they help you bring this movie into the  world may you inspire us deeply again  with the screenplay that you're gonna  write this year as a nickel feller  [Laughter]  [Applause]  [Applause]  [Laughter]  hi you guys so well that was that was  really great thank you  cuz that was fun okay  first off congratulations to my fellow  fellows it's been an incredible week and  I'm so happy we got to experience it  together and make memories that will  look back on forever  congratulations you guys that's great  I'd like to thank the Academy the nickel  committee and this year's judges for  championing championing my work and  keeps keep saying yes all of your kind  words support and love for Horsehead  Girls has humbled me and given me a  sense of pride and accomplishment thanks  to Joan Chris and Jacob you have been  our patient guides throughout this past  month and especially this week you love  your job and it really shows thank you  to my amazing family my husband Edie  my boys Cooper McCallister and Benjamin  and thanks to my friends and family that  loved and encouraged me you know who you  are and I love you all so much  it says breathe here  November is Native American Heritage  Month and to tell you about myself as a  writer I have to tell you about my own  Native American heritage the first  screenplay I wrote was based on my  grandmother's childhood she was a Dakota  Indian in 1926 at the age of five she  was kidnapped from her reservation by  two strange men in suits thrown into the  back of a car and taken across state  lines to a mandated government-run  Indian boarding school she had no  contact with her parents for the next  seven years she suffered many forms of  abuse neglect and isolation and was  systematically stripped of her culture  and family and life as she knew it  anyone that knows a five year old think  about that people love that script they  said your story needs to be told this is  your history your voice should be heard  and then followed by but nobody will  watch a film with a female native  protagonist you have no audience no  actors and no investors lesson learned I  spent the next 16 years writing about  zombies aliens and highschool girls and  I worked on being a better writer a  better storyteller and better prepared  to stand for and write about something I  believe in that's when I learned about  the missing and murdered indigenous  women movement and I knew what I had to  do now 18 years after that first script  Here I am once again writing about  Native American girls being kidnapped  abused and forgotten it hasn't stopped  it never stopped but this time is  different  today I'm hearing this story will be  told this is our history and your voice  is being heard because there is an  audience and we will find actors and  people want to help and the weight and  the work and the patience was all worth  it and today I'm able to honor my  grandmother her mother and all the  people who are missing someone taken  from them her name was Katherine and  this is for her McGlinn
 

